= Perception =

== Overview ==
'''Perception''' is the set of processes by which organisms organize and interpret sensory information to represent or guide interaction with their environment.

Perception depends on sensory input, but it is not a passive copy of stimulation. Prior knowledge, expectations, goals, action, and context can influence how signals are interpreted.

== Sensation and perception ==
'''Sensation''' concerns detection and transduction of physical stimulation by sensory systems.

'''Perception''' concerns organization and interpretation of that information.

The distinction is useful, but biological processing is continuous and does not always divide cleanly into a sensation stage followed by a perception stage.

== Bottom-up and top-down influences ==
Bottom-up processing is driven strongly by incoming signal structure.

Top-down processing uses expectations, knowledge, goals, or context.

Most real perception combines both.

== Perceptual organization ==
Perception involves grouping features, estimating depth and motion, identifying objects, integrating information across senses, maintaining constancy, and resolving ambiguous input.

[[Attention]] can prioritize some information over other information, while [[Salience]] can make particular features more competitive for processing.

== Perception and action ==
Perception guides action.

Embodied and ecological approaches emphasize perception–action loops rather than treating perception as an isolated internal picture of the world.

[[Embodied cognition]] develops this relationship more broadly.

== Illusions ==
Perceptual illusions are systematic differences between stimulus properties and perceptual experience or judgment.

They can reveal the assumptions and computations normally used to resolve uncertain sensory information.

